A||N||3||gkozlov||86 SE questions||||5||
Z||000000||gkozlov||05-31-2000||07:43 AM||||I have a 86 SE V-6 4spd.<BR>1. My shifter is very sloppy. Difficult to find gears. Any way to fix/ tighten this?<BR>2. Any way to install 87 or 88 headlight motors to avoid expensive pre 87 repair bills?<P>                    Thanks in advance<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||DJRice||05-31-2000||09:38 AM||||Shifter:  You might check your shifter cables to see if they are worn.  Also check the bracket that holds them to tranny, as I have heard mention of these loosening up over time.  Check the slave cylinder also to make sure that it is not loose.  It seems to me that by 86, Pontiac had upgraded to the steel clutch pedal, but I dont remember for sure.  If a magnet will not stick to clutch pedal, then it is aluminum, and could very well be bent.  A bent pedal can feel like a lot of different problems.<P>Headlights:  You can change them, but you will need both headlight motors and the entire lighting wiring harness from the 87-88 donor car.  If you can find the wiring harness by itself, you can get the motors from any 87-92? firebird or vette as they all use the same motors.  Rebuild kits for those are about $5.  Kick hill may have a wiring harness available.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||05-31-2000||09:40 AM||||By lighting wiring harness I mean just the forward wiring harness from front bulkhead to headlights....its not a big deal to replace.  <P>I didnt want you to think you had to rewire the lights in the entire car...||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||05-31-2000||10:32 AM||||DJRice mentioned some good things to check. Those 4 speeds are know for sloppy shifters. Often it's the nylon bushings on the cable ends at the tranny that are worn out. Rodney Dickman either has those replacements or will soon have them. Another thing to check is the shifter adjustment that you have to remove the console to get to.||||reg||1
